   observed; the article's own later sentence ("Keeping the routing line for
   good may be open, while trying it with a cheap way back can be better
   under every compatible measure") and the reversibility note it links. The
   must-keep claim that accepting a trial selects a modified proposal and
   the unit-of-learning link are kept.
2. Before: "Those reasons can be checked and challenged one at a time."
   After: "The factual parts of those reasons, such as which work changed,
   can be checked one at a time. The weighing of whose costs count cannot be
   checked independently; it can only be challenged." Evidence:
   `necessary-preference-can-guide-improvement-without-a-global-measure.md`,
   which says the factual parts can be checked independently of who offered
   them and the weighing cannot.
3. Before: "… the system sits on the computation side of the Bitter Lesson."
   After: "… the system sits on the computation side of the Bitter Lesson if
   its results improve as more computation is spent." Evidence: the same
   paragraph says whether that spending pays is open, and the bootstrap
   condition below it; nothing in the article shows scaling.
4. Before: "Unlike proof-gated switches, empirical admission admits harmful
   changes, and …" After: "A proof gate admits a switch only when it is
   proved better under stated axioms; empirical admission certifies only
   that a change passed the checks it faced. A harmful change that passes
   those checks is admitted, and …" Evidence: the Gödel paragraph says a
   valid proof does not establish that the axioms describe the domain, so a
   proof gate does not certify harmlessness either; the old contrast implied
   it did.
5. Before: "A model that approves a change and then approves its reversal on
   the same evidence is equivalent to no set of value functions". After: "A
   model that strictly prefers a change and then, on the same evidence,
   strictly prefers its reversal matches no fixed set of value functions".
   Evidence: weak approval of both directions is consistent with a tie, and
   possible preferences can run both ways under one set; only strict
   preferences in both directions under one fixed set are inconsistent.
6. Before: "Pre-training by gradient descent works the same way." After: "A
   plain gradient step in pre-training works the same way. … A training run
   that also keeps or discards checkpoints adds selection on top of those
   steps." Evidence: the article's own later sentence that a model whose
   checkpoint is picked by its engineers learns by selection, and the
   AlphaGo Zero example in the same section.

Noticed, not applied: the third point of the illustration summary ("no single
